0. This repository is public. That is not a licence.
----------------------------------------------------

The source is visible so that a prospective or existing customer can read it,
audit it, and see exactly what a service they run on their own machine does —
which matters more here than elsewhere, because this binary is BUILT on that
machine rather than shipped to it. Visibility is deliberate and it grants
nothing: absent a Commercial Agreement under section 2, you may read this
repository and you may not use it.

Said plainly here because the opposite is the common assumption. A public
repository with no licence file is "all rights reserved" by silence, which
leaves everyone guessing; this file replaces the guess with a statement.

1. Ownership
------------

This software, including its source code, build scripts, configuration,
documentation and all other material in this repository (the "Software"), is
the proprietary property of the copyright holder and is protected by copyright
and other intellectual property laws.

The Software is licensed, not sold. No title to or ownership of the Software is
transferred by this licence.

2. No licence granted by default
--------------------------------

No rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense or sell
the Software are granted except under a separate written agreement signed by
the copyright holder (a "Commercial Agreement"). Absent a Commercial Agreement,
you may not use the Software for any purpose.

Where a Commercial Agreement exists, that agreement governs, and this file is
subordinate to it.

3. Restrictions
---------------

Except to the extent such a restriction is unenforceable under applicable law,
or is expressly permitted by a Commercial Agreement, you may not:

  (a) distribute, publish, sublicense, rent, lease, lend or host the Software
      for the benefit of any third party;
  (b) reverse engineer, decompile or disassemble the Software;
  (c) remove, obscure or alter any copyright, trademark or attribution notice,
      including the notices in NOTICE and THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md;
  (d) use the name of the copyright holder or the "DewFlow" name or marks for
      endorsement or publicity.

4. Third-party components
-------------------------

The Software incorporates third-party open-source components, each governed by
its own licence. Those licences apply to those components and are not
superseded by this file. Attribution and licence terms for every third-party
component are recorded in:

  - NOTICE                     — attribution carried with any distribution
  - THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md     — the per-component inventory, with versions

Three facts about this repository's position, each resolved from the artefacts
rather than assumed, and each different from the rest of the family:

  (i)   `vendor-fastembed/` is a FULL VENDORED COPY of the upstream `fastembed`
        crate (Apache-2.0), carrying one marked patch. Its licence travels with
        it and is not superseded by this file.

  (ii)  One shipped dependency is MPL-2.0 — `option-ext`, reached through
        dirs → hf-hub → fastembed. MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft: it obliges
        us to make ITS source available and to release any modification of ITS
        files under MPL. We modify nothing in it. THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
        records this and where the source is.

  (iii) The GPU execution providers — Microsoft DirectML, NVIDIA CUDA and
        cuDNN — are USED and NEVER REDISTRIBUTED. They are vendor-licensed,
        and one cached copy in our registry, an image layer or an offline
        bundle would make us a redistributor. This is why the customer's own
        machine builds the flavour it needs, and why no build here bundles a
        vendor provider. See THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md § "Never redistributed".

Nothing in this file limits any right you have under the licence of a
third-party component.

